Confidence: ~10% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: PKIX-q74z.5 (TBS round-trip canonicalization is hard to falsify without running against PKITS / limbo / live-internet corpora); PKIX-q74z.11 (the timing side-channel scenario is plausibly worse than I sketched; would need a peer review by someone who has worked on Merkle proof timing attacks). Uncertainty driven by: lack of differential testing against real CT logs at scale (only one captured triple in live_log.rs), and no fuzz harness on the SCT parser. Would raise confidence if: (1) PKITS/limbo run on tbs_without_sct_list confirming round-trip is universal, (2) cargo-fuzz harness on SctList::from_extension_value + SignedCertificateTimestamp::from_bytes ran for 24h with no crashes, (3) the SctVerifier code was reviewed by another set of eyes against RFC 6962 / RFC 9162 line by line.
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — clean parser, sound algorithms, generous independent-oracle test discipline, but several public-API and trust-boundary issues (Precert Signing Certificate handling, OCSP-status conflation, silent log-state acceptance) need fixing before 1.0 to avoid CVE class issues post-launch.
